---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SavingsCalculator from '@components/islands/SavingsCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'savings-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How much will my savings grow?', answer: 'In "Project savings" mode, enter your starting balance, monthly deposit, expected return and time frame, and the calculator projects your future balance and how much of it is interest.' },
  { question: 'How much do I need to save each month for a goal?', answer: 'Switch to "Reach a savings goal" mode, enter your target amount and time frame, and the calculator solves for the monthly deposit required — accounting for your starting balance and expected growth.' },
  { question: 'How much of my income should I save?', answer: 'A common guideline is the 50/30/20 rule: roughly 50% of take-home pay for needs, 30% for wants and 20% for saving and debt repayment. Saving 20% is a solid target, but any consistent amount beats none.' },
  { question: 'How big should my emergency fund be?', answer: 'Most guidance suggests three to six months of essential expenses, kept in an accessible, low-risk account such as a high-yield savings account. Build this first, before longer-term investing, so a surprise cost does not become debt.' },
  { question: 'What return rate should I use?', answer: 'A high-yield savings account might return 3–5%, while long-term investments have historically returned more with more risk. Use a rate that matches where you will actually keep the money.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Project how your savings grow with regular deposits, or work backwards to find the monthly amount needed to reach a specific goal."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-22' }}
>
  <SavingsCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick a mode: <strong>Project savings</strong> to grow deposits forward, or <strong>Reach a savings goal</strong> to solve for the deposit you need.</li>
      <li>In Project mode, enter your <strong>starting balance</strong>, <strong>monthly deposit</strong>, expected <strong>annual return</strong> and number of <strong>years</strong>.</li>
      <li>In Goal mode, enter your <strong>savings goal</strong>, starting balance, return and years instead.</li>
      <li>Read the <strong>projected balance</strong> (or the <strong>required monthly deposit</strong>) and the split between what you put in and what growth added.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Two ways to plan</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Project savings</strong> shows where a consistent habit will take you — future
      balance, total deposited and interest earned. <strong>Reach a savings goal</strong> flips
      the question: name a target and a timeline, and it returns the monthly deposit required,
      after crediting the growth on your starting balance. Both assume deposits are made monthly
      with monthly compounding.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>
      Projecting forward combines the growth of your starting balance with the future value of a
      stream of monthly deposits:
    </p>
    <p><strong>FV = P(1 + i)<sup>n</sup> + D · ((1 + i)<sup>n</sup> − 1) / i</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>FV</strong> — the projected future balance</li>
      <li><strong>P</strong> — your starting balance</li>
      <li><strong>D</strong> — the monthly deposit</li>
      <li><strong>i</strong> — the monthly return (annual rate ÷ 12)</li>
      <li><strong>n</strong> — the number of months (years × 12)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Goal mode rearranges the same equation to solve for <em>D</em>: it grows your starting
      balance to the target date, subtracts that from the goal, and divides the shortfall by the
      annuity factor ((1 + i)<sup>n</sup> − 1) / i.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example: projecting forward</h2>
    <p>
      Save <strong>$500 a month</strong> in an account earning <strong>4%</strong> for
      <strong>5 years</strong>, starting from zero. You finish with about
      <strong>$33,149</strong> — roughly <strong>$30,000</strong> of deposits plus
      <strong>$3,149</strong> of interest. Push the same habit to <strong>10 years</strong> and
      the balance grows to about <strong>$73,625</strong>: the deposits double to $60,000, but the
      interest more than quadruples to about $13,625, because each dollar has longer to compound.
    </p>

    <h2>Solving for a goal</h2>
    <p>
      Now flip it: you want <strong>$30,000</strong> in <strong>5 years</strong> at
      <strong>4%</strong>, starting from zero. The calculator returns a required deposit of about
      <strong>$452.50 a month</strong>. Begin with a <strong>$5,000</strong> balance instead and
      that starting sum grows to about $6,105 on its own, cutting the required deposit to roughly
      <strong>$360.41 a month</strong> — a concrete illustration of how a head start does part of
      the work for you.
    </p>

    <h2>How much should you save?</h2>
    <p>
      Two rules of thumb make a plan concrete. The <strong>50/30/20 rule</strong> puts about
      50% of take-home pay toward needs, 30% toward wants and 20% toward saving and debt. And an
      <strong>emergency fund</strong> of three to six months of essential expenses comes first, in
      an accessible account, before longer-term investing — it is what keeps a surprise bill from
      turning into debt.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a realistic return</h2>
    <p>
      Use a rate that matches where the money will actually sit. A high-yield savings account
      might return roughly 3–5%, while long-term investments have historically returned more with
      more risk and more year-to-year swings. Cash you may need within a year or two belongs in a
      safe, accessible account; only money you can leave untouched for years suits higher-return,
      higher-volatility investments.
    </p>

    <h2>Tips and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automate it.</strong> A fixed transfer on payday is the single most reliable savings habit — you never have to decide to save.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole years and monthly deposits.</strong> The projection assumes monthly deposits, monthly compounding and whole years, at one steady rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Before tax and inflation.</strong> Interest in a taxable account is usually taxed, and results are in today's nominal dollars, so real buying power grows more slowly.</li>
      <li><strong>Rates change.</strong> Savings rates move over time, so revisit the projection as your actual rate shifts.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      To model longer-term, higher-return investing with a contributions-versus-earnings split,
      see the <a href="/finance/investment-calculator">investment calculator</a>; to explore how
      compounding frequency changes growth, use the
      <a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">compound interest calculator</a>. This is
      general educational information, not financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
